IMPORT prelude

-- Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), Schedule 2 and Parts IIIA and V -- the dates, in one small module.
--
-- Built-in date functions only (DATE_FROM_DMY, DATE_SERIAL, DATE_FROM_SERIAL, DATE_DAY, DATE_MONTH, DATE_YEAR), not the
-- daydate library. Schedule 2 counts periods in years (cl 14: "1 year after", "3 years after", "6 years after", the 21st
-- birthday; age 18), s 52A in days ("within 14 days after") and ss 25(2), 25A(4) in years ("within 6 years of the day").
--
-- Sourced from the Federal Register of Legislation, Commonwealth of Australia, CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Dates for the Privacy Act 1988 baseline of November 2026`

GIVEN d IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the calendar day` d m y MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY d m y

-- Plain days from one day to another (negative if the second is earlier).
GIVEN `the start` IS A DATE, `the end` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of days from` `the start` `until` `the end` MEANS DATE_SERIAL `the end` MINUS DATE_SERIAL `the start`

-- The day a number of days after a day ("within 60 days after X": the last day is X plus 60).
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, `the days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the day` `plus days` `the days` MEANS DATE_FROM_SERIAL (DATE_SERIAL `the day` PLUS `the days`)

-- Whether the first day is strictly before the second.
GIVEN `the first` IS A DATE, `the second` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the first` `is earlier than` `the second` IF DATE_SERIAL `the first` LESS THAN DATE_SERIAL `the second`

GIVEN y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a leap year` y IF
        y MODULO 4 EQUALS 0
    AND (    (NOT (y MODULO 100 EQUALS 0))
         OR  y MODULO 400 EQUALS 0)

GIVEN m IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days in month` m y MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF m EQUALS 2 AND `is a leap year` y THEN 29
        IF m EQUALS 2 THEN 28
        IF m EQUALS 4 OR m EQUALS 6 OR m EQUALS 9 OR m EQUALS 11 THEN 30
        OTHERWISE 31

-- The day a number of calendar months after a day: the same day of the month that many months on. Where that month has
-- no such day (31 January plus one month; 29 February plus 12 or 24 months in a common year) it is the last day of that
-- month (fork F1 in NOTES.md).
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, `the months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the day` `plus calendar months` `the months` MEANS
    DATE_FROM_DMY `the day of the month` `the month` `the year`
    WHERE
        `the count` MEANS (DATE_YEAR `the day` TIMES 12) PLUS (DATE_MONTH `the day` MINUS 1) PLUS `the months`
        `the month index` MEANS `the count` MODULO 12
        `the month` MEANS `the month index` PLUS 1
        `the year` MEANS (`the count` MINUS `the month index`) DIVIDED BY 12
        `the last day` MEANS `the days in month` `the month` `the year`
        `the day of the month` MEANS IF DATE_DAY `the day` GREATER THAN `the last day` THEN `the last day` ELSE DATE_DAY `the day`

-- The day a number of whole years after a day (the anniversary), with the same month-end rule (fork F1).
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, `the years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the day` `plus calendar years` `the years` MEANS `the day` `plus calendar months` (`the years` TIMES 12)

-- The day before a day ("before the day that is 1 year after ...": the last day is the day before that day).
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day before` `the day` MEANS DATE_FROM_SERIAL (DATE_SERIAL `the day` MINUS 1)

-- The earlier of two days.
GIVEN `the first` IS A DATE, `the second` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the earlier of` `the first` `and` `the second` MEANS IF `the first` `is earlier than` `the second` THEN `the first` ELSE `the second`

-- Days from a start day to a later day, never below 0 (a day given before the start is counted as day 0).
GIVEN `the end` IS A DATE, `the start` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the day number of` `the end` `counted from` `the start` MEANS
    IF `the raw count` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw count`
    WHERE
        `the raw count` MEANS `the number of days from` `the start` `until` `the end`
